{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom/fold.go at exact bfcd6683bff87a993917aff027d77017a50fbcef on request/incremental-unsettled, under promise git:sha1…8e6ea2c on request git:sha1…a3a28d3\n\nImplementation of request d1b3d1b1 at head bfcd6683b (two commits on main b81a80cf0). Before this change, each merge receipt with left-live testimony computed staleness for the whole log and then projected every commitment, to find the commitments that still expect a move. Staleness never decides which commitment rows exist or which request, promise and report events they carry; it only turns an open or promised row with nothing reported into a stale one, and all three statuses count as unsettled. So the fold now projects the commitments without staleness, at the same capture point, and the projection's cleanup count uses the same function. This removes the whole-graph staleness pass from every receipt. Each receipt still projects every commitment, so the remaining capture cost still grows with the log. Changed: internal/workroom/fold.go (layer 5 receipt admission, layer 6 cleanup accounting) and its test.\nCondition 1: on the frozen artroom copy (depth 43,744, 199 receipts) the capture was 83-85 % of a cold fold, 4.8-5.4 s of 5.7-6.4 s fold CPU (pprof, three runs). After: 0.6-0.7 s, 43-45 % of a fold that costs 1.4-1.6 s CPU. One cold gs status --json --server - falls from 14.9-16.0 s to 9.5-9.9 s wall on the artroom copy, and from 13.1 s to 9.3 s on a copy of the gitseq log. An independent review harness measured the fold alone at 6.93 s to 2.02 s (artroom) and 8.71 s to 4.40 s (gitseq log).\nCondition 2: TestReceiptUnsettledCaptureMatchesTheStalenessAwareAnswer folds a fixture one record at a time and, before every record, requires the captured set to equal the earlier staleness-aware answer, kept word for word in the test. The fixture has three capturing receipts, retirements with and without successors, review promises withdrawn before and after their merges, and five commitments that go stale: an open request, a promise with nothing reported, a request answered by a direct report, a promise with an unratified report and a promise with a ratified report; the test requires all five stale, with their completions and statuses, at every receipt frontier. Six mutations each turn it red: a direct report dropped from a stale request, staleness overriding a completed promise row, a promise row's completion dropped when stale, either stale status moved outside the unsettled list, and a set reused across the fold. Moving the capture after the receipt turns two existing receipt-protection tests red.\nCondition 3: projections are byte-identical to main b81a80cf0's on the artroom copy (sha256 a7122350...) and on a copy of the gitseq log at depth 24,947 (sha256 3eb683dc...), confirmed again by the independent review harness. The second commit changes only a comment in fold.go and the test.\nCondition 4: no persisted format, cache, checkpoint, profile or trust change. architecture.md's statement that the fold captures the unsettled set once before appending the receipt still holds, so it is unchanged.\nCondition 5: go test -timeout 40m ./... passed at bfcd6683b, 36 packages.","body":{"commit":"bfcd6683bff87a993917aff027d77017a50fbcef","path":"internal/workroom/fold.go"}}